+++
title: Our view on coaching
+++

Therapy, mentoring, and coaching sit on the same rough scale of people who help you move forward, but they pull in different directions. A therapist often helps you understand and process the past, excavating wounds to heal them. A mentor has usually done the thing you’re trying to do, passing on what they learned in the trenches. A coach does neither by default. A coach asks the questions that help you find your own answer, and holds you accountable to the steps you decide on yourself. The work is forward-facing; it is about building capacity rather than repairing what is broken.

In practice, however, the work sits a little toward the mentoring side of pure coaching. Someone who already knows exactly what they want mostly needs the accountability and the outside perspective—that is coaching in its purest form. But plenty of people arrive only knowing that something isn’t working, not yet able to name what they actually want instead. Helping someone get clear on that comes first when it’s needed, so the coaching that follows is pointed at something real instead of a guess. We treat this as an intentional experiment: observe what is happening, try a new approach, reflect on the results, and integrate the lessons. The methodology serves the moment, not the other way around. Whether we are peeling back a limiting belief or optimizing a performance habit, the focus remains on your forward trajectory.

What actually determines whether an engagement helps has less to do with methodology or domain than most people assume, and more to do with the relationship itself. Carl Rogers made essentially this argument in 1957, proposing that a small set of relational conditions—empathy, genuineness, and unconditional positive regard—were the necessary and sufficient conditions for real change, more than any particular technique (Rogers, C. R., “The Necessary and Sufficient Conditions of Therapeutic Personality Change,” Journal of Consulting Psychology, 1957). The research since has largely held up: across the psychotherapy literature, the strength of the working relationship—assessed as early as the first few sessions—is one of the most consistent predictors of outcome there is, repeatedly found to matter as much as or more than which specific approach was used (see, among others, Horvath & Symonds, “Relation Between Working Alliance and Outcome in Psychotherapy: A Meta-Analysis,” Journal of Counseling Psychology, 1991; and Martin, Garske & Davis, “Relation of the Therapeutic Alliance With Outcome and Other Variables: A Meta-Analytic Review,” Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology, 2000). This isn’t just academic theory; it’s the operating system of our practice.

That’s the real reason behind the introductory conversation described in our terms. Before we accept payment for any engagement, we ask a prospective client to speak with their prospective coach first. We’re not screening for a good pitch; we’re checking, together, whether this particular pairing is one where real work can actually happen. Only if you come away feeling aligned with, and believed in by that coach will we accept a paid engagement. If it doesn’t feel that way, we’d rather say so before any money changes hands than after.

Most of us are non-traditionalists who believe in experimentation and continual learning. We are versed in reflective inquiry, which is often held as a core part of coaching conversations, but we also bring a more diverse skill set into the conversation. Depending on where you are, tools from NLP, hypnosis, and narrative coaching can be brought out when you’re looking for more than just situational unblocking and want to get into deeper personal transformation. We avoid acting as knowledge gatekeepers, preferring instead to offer interactive discussion and curated resources that help you discover your authentic self. 

Where it’s right for you, in-person sessions can include elements to facilitate emotional openness, moving beyond the standard desk setup. A walk in nature, sharing a light lunch, or the inclusion of massage are all tools to lower defences. The goal is to stimulate your production of oxytocin, to help you connect, feel safe, and allow you to be more receptive to the ideas that coaching is uncovering within you. When the environment supports the work, the mind follows naturally.

Coaching doesn’t require something to be wrong within you. Coaching shouldn't be a last resort and isn't a sign you couldn’t figure it out alone. Coaching is a normal thing capable people seek out on purpose, the same way serious athletes train with a coach to drive improvement. Michael Jordan, at the height of being the best basketball player alive, still sought out coaching for himself, not only from the team’s coaching staff, but he engaged his own personal coaches, by choice. Even someone at that level still believed he needed several coaches in his corner. To live a fully realized life, or to perform at the highest level requires an external eye, a trusted voice to catch the blind spots you can’t see yourself. Anyone who wants to get somewhere can benefit from having someone to talk about their progress with, to stand outside and help examine strengths, weaknesses, and growth. Those who seek out meaningful feedback, and who reflect on and incorporate that feedback into what they do and how they do it, are those who grow.

We back our beliefs with a real commitment, not just an empty sentiment. Each coach here carries a small number of pro-bono clients, so that lacking the money to engage us isn’t what stands between an individual and their growth. That capacity isn’t spread evenly across every domain, though—leadership coaching, for instance, generally serves people already established enough in their career to be leading something in the first place, so it isn’t part of this pool. Our limited pro-bono capacity is directed instead toward the domains where financial means is more plausibly the actual barrier standing between someone and the growth they’re after. We want access to be driven by need and alignment, not just the bottom line. When resources are tight, we try to make space for the work to happen anyway.

Sessions are built around whatever actually impacting you right now. Some people come with a specific goal, ready to sprint. Some come because something feels stuck and they can’t yet name what, ready to wander and discover. Both are a reasonable place to start. The structure follows the need. You bring your life, your goals, and your questions; we provide the space, the perspective, and the accountability to move forward. We treat the coaching engagement as a dedicated area to step out of the noise of daily life and focus entirely on your trajectory. It is a partnership in movement, guided by curiosity and grounded in trust. At Yomonsni, we don’t just talk about change, we create the conditions for it to happen, one intentional step at a time.

Coaching is not a quick fix solution to every situation. We can't guarantee you any particular outcome, nor will we enforce you doing the work for your growth. You will always completely own the work required to move toward what you want.

